sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch11-15> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Many more identical and allied plant forms migrated from north to south during the Glacial period than in the reverse direction, because the greater land area of the north and the larger populations there had made northern forms more advanced and dominant through natural selection and competition.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Glacial interchange ran mainly north to south because northern forms were more dominant";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XI, “Geographical Distribution”; section “Dispersal during the Glacial period co-extensive with the world”. Scope: plants migrating between hemispheres during the Glacial period"
}
